// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries and conjectures of topology, MSC 2020 54
// (general topology), 55 (algebraic topology), 57 (manifolds, knots) and 58A (smooth manifolds, differential forms). In
// English. Built on 3 November 2026. Moved here by rule 1 of ../../CLAUDE.md, with their ids: `jordan-curve-theorem`,
// `classification-of-surfaces` and `borsuk-ulam-theorem` from ../../combinatorics/ (there foundations without
// premises), `hodge-theorem` and `thom-cobordism` from ../../algebraic-geometry/, `decomposition-theorem` from
// ../../representation-theory/.
//
// Model as in ../../algebraic-number-theory/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are
// the standard textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a
// proof that rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`). `area` is the three-character MSC class, as in
// data/concepts.js. `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// References to theorems of other folders: "st:" (supremum property, Zorn's lemma), "gt:", "ca:", "ct:" (long exact
// homology sequence, five lemma, chain homotopies), "ag:" (Deligne's purity theorem), and "ana:" (analysis: inverse
// function theorem, Fubini, Taylor, fundamental theorem of calculus, Picard–Lindelöf, Riemann mapping theorem; ids
// expected in the analysis folder that is being built at the same time).
//
// Sources: Munkres, Topology (2nd ed. 2000); Hatcher, Algebraic Topology (2002); Bredon, Topology and Geometry (1993);
// Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds (2nd ed. 2013); Milnor, Topology from the Differentiable Viewpoint (1965);
// Milnor, Morse Theory (1963) and Lectures on the h-Cobordism Theorem (1965); Bott and Tu, Differential Forms in
// Algebraic Topology (1982); Lickorish, An Introduction to Knot Theory (1997). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

